public class StateLoader {

    public static MoSTState loadMoSTState(String filePath, ConfigurationService configService) {
        try (ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(new FileInputStream(filePath))) {
            byte[] fileBytes = ((ByteArrayOutputStream) ois.readObject()).toByteArray();
            long expectedChecksum = configService.getExpectedChecksum();
            long actualChecksum = calculateChecksum(fileBytes);

            if (actualChecksum != expectedChecksum) {
                System.err.println("Checksum mismatch. Expected: " + expectedChecksum + ", but got: " + actualChecksum);
            }

            Object obj = ois.readObject();
            if (obj instanceof MoSTState) {
                return (MoSTState) obj;
            }
        } catch (FileNotFoundException e) {
            System.err.println("File not found: " + filePath);
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.err.println("Error reading file or deserializing object.");
        }

        return null;
    }

    private static long calculateChecksum(byte[] bytes) {
        Adler32 adler = new Adler32();
        adler.update(bytes, 0, bytes.length);
        return adler.getValue();
    }
}
```

This code snippet defines a `StateLoader` class with a static method `loadMoSTState` that takes a file path and a configuration service as parameters. The method attempts to load a state object from the specified file path using an `ObjectInputStream`. It first reads the entire file into memory, calculates its checksum, compares it against the expected value retrieved from the configuration service, and then deserializes the object if it is an instance of `MoSTState`.

The method handles various exceptions such as `FileNotFoundException`, `IOException`, and `ClassNotFoundException` gracefully. It uses a `try-with-resources` statement to ensure that the `ObjectInputStream` is closed properly in all execution paths.

Note: The `ConfigurationService` interface and its implementation are not provided here, as they are assumed to be part of the external configuration service that can retrieve the expected checksum value dynamically.']

